+++
title: Old Assyrian period
description: Second period of Assyrian history
key-facts:
  - name: The city it is all about
    value: Assur
  - name: Assur becomes independent from the Third Dynasty of Ur
    value: c. 2025 BC
  - name: Shamshi-Adad I conquers the city
    value: c. 1808 BC
  - name: The Adaside dynasty is founded
    value: c. 1700 BC
  - name: Subjugation under Mitanni
    value: c. 1430–1360 BC
  - name: Languages spoken
    value: Akkadian, Sumerian and Amorite
image-plan:
  - file: KültepeUnterstadt1.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: The excavated ruins of the Old Assyrian trading colony at Kültepe,
      roughly 1,000 kilometers (620 miles) from Assur — about six weeks by
      donkey caravan
  - file: Cuneiform_tablet_case_impressed_with_four_cylinder_seals_Old_Assyrian.png
    role: inline
    section: trading-colonies
    caption: "A loan repaid, and recorded: an Old Assyrian tablet from Kültepe,
      its case impressed with four different cylinder seals"
  - file: Itur-ili_-_Business_Letter_-_Walters_481462_-_View_A.jpg
    role: inline
    section: population-and-culture
    caption: An Old Assyrian letter found at Kültepe, concerning the trade of
      precious metals
  - file: Samsi_Addu.PNG
    role: full
    section: conquests-of-shamshi-adad
    caption: The approximate extent of the short-lived Kingdom of Upper
      Mesopotamia c. 1776 BC, founded by the Amorite conqueror Shamshi-Adad I,
      who deposed the Assyrian royal dynasty Puzur-Ashur I had founded
  - file: Babylone_1.PNG
    role: full
    section: collapse-of-the-kingdom
    caption: The Old Babylonian Empire under Hammurabi (c. 1792–1750 BC), which
      briefly controlled Assur in the aftermath of the collapse of
      Shamshi-Adad's kingdom
  - file: KAV_14.jpg
    role: inline
    section: assyrian-dark-age
    caption: "Line-art of KAV 14, a fragmentary version of the Assyrian King List:
      it records Ishme-Dagan I's successors as his descendants Mut-Ashkur and
      Rimush, figures who do not appear in the standard version of the list"
  - file: Ancient_Near_East_1400BC.svg
    role: full
    section: rise-of-assyria
    caption: "The Ancient Near East in 1400 BC, approximately: Assur was during
      this time subjugated by the Mitanni kingdom"
  - file: Drinking_vessel_in_the_shape_of_a_woman's_head._From_Assur,_Iraq._1500-1200_BCE._British_Museum.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeological-evidence
    caption: A drinking vessel in the shape of a woman's head, from Assur,
      1500–1200 BC
  - file: Furniture_element_with_a_monkey_MET_DP110501.jpg
    role: inline
    section: old-assyrian-families
    caption: A furniture element depicting a monkey, from the Old Assyrian trading
      colony at Kültepe
  - file: Cuneiform_tablet-_commercial_note_concerning_caravan_expenses_MET_ME66_245_13.jpg
    role: inline
    section: slavery
    caption: Expenses on a caravan journey, recorded on a tablet from Kültepe;
      some male slaves worked the international trade as caravan personnel
  - file: Clay_tablet_and_its_stamped_envelop._Legal_debt-note_on_silver._Old_Assyrian_dialect._Assyrian_Colony_Period,_19th_century_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum,_Istanbul.jpg
    role: inline
    section: economy-and-trade
    caption: A legal debt-note on silver from Kültepe, where loan contracts are
      among the most common tablets recovered
  - file: Old_Assyrian_drinking_vessel_Kültepe.png
    role: inline
    section: cuisine-and-diet
    caption: An Old Assyrian drinking vessel found at Kültepe, in the shape of a
      ram's head
  - file: Cuneiform_tablet-_private_letter_MET_DP-13441-003.jpg
    role: inline
    section: language
    caption: A private letter, in Old Assyrian, on a tablet from Kültepe
  - file: Cult_wall_relief_from_Assur._A_deity,_probably_god_Assur,_is_flanked_by_2_water_deities_and_2_goats._2000-1500_BCE._Pergamon_Museum,_Berlin.jpg
    role: full
    section: religion
    caption: Wall relief from Assur, 2000–1500 BC, depicting a deity, probably
      Ashur, flanked by two water deities and two goats
  - file: Cylinder_seal_Old_Assyrian_22963_(detail).jpg
    role: full
    section: religion
    caption: "Three deities approach a seated figure, probably a king: the storm
      god holding his lightning fork and standing on the back of a bull, the
      moon god holding a crescent standard and standing in a boat, and an
      interceding goddess. Old Assyrian period, ca 1920–1740 BC. British
      Museum ME 22963."
further:
  - slug: early-assyrian-period
    why: The much more poorly known period before this one, when Assur was not
      independent but part of a sequence of states and empires from southern
      Mesopotamia — and when the god Ashur and the worship of Ishtar at Assur
      probably originated.
  - slug: middle-assyrian-empire
    why: "What comes next: the territorial state that begins with Ashur-uballit I,
      the first native Assyrian ruler to claim the dignity of king rather than
      governor."
  - slug: assyria
    why: The whole of Assyrian history, of which the Old Assyrian period is the
      second stage.
